<main>
<header class="top">
  <h1>E-commerce analytics pipeline</h1>
  <div class="lede">From nine raw Olist files to tested, documented marts that give one answer to “how many orders?”, and the analysis those marts make possible.</div>
  <div class="meta">Generated {{ generated }} from commit {{ commit }} · data: Olist Brazilian e-commerce (Kaggle), orders 2016-09 to 2018-10 · dbt Core {{ stats.dbt_version }} on DuckDB · repo: github.com/edwinzhang98/ecommerce-analytics-pipeline</div>
</header>

<section class="tldr">
  <strong>TL;DR</strong>
  <ul>
    <li><b>The problem was real and explainable.</b> On the raw tables “orders” came out as {{ n.fanout_rows|int }}, {{ n.placed|int }} or {{ n.delivered|int }}: joins multiplied rows, and analysts used different definitions.</li>
    <li><b>Now there is one number.</b> {{ n.net|int }} net orders (placed minus canceled and unavailable), with placed and delivered published under their own names. Every query path through the marts agrees, and a six-path dbt test fails the build if that ever changes.</li>
    <li><b>Trust is tested, not claimed.</b> {{ stats.data_tests + stats.unit_tests }} tests pass on every build, including {{ stats.known_issue_tests }} baselines that watch the {{ stats.dq_issues }} documented raw-data issues. CI runs on every pull request.</li>
    <li><b>The marts answer real questions</b> (associations, not causes):
      <ul>
        <li>Late deliveries draw 1-2 star reviews {{ n.bad_late|pct }} of the time vs {{ n.bad_ok|pct }} on time.</li>
        <li>Only {{ n.repeat|pct(2) }} of customers ever buy again on a different day.</li>
        <li>The top 10% of sellers hold {{ n.top10|pct }} of net GMV.</li>
        <li>Late seller handover goes with {{ n.hv_late|pct }} late deliveries vs {{ n.hv_ok|pct }}.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>

