<h2 id="problem">1. The problem</h2>
<div class="question">The Analytics Lead: “Three analysts got three different order counts, and the business no longer trusts our numbers. Build trusted, tested, documented marts so analysts query only marts, never raw.”</div>
<div class="fig" id="fig-three">{{ figs.three | safe }}</div>
<ul>
  <li><b>Analyst A</b> joined orders to items and payments before counting: one order with 3 items and 2 payments becomes 6 rows. The same query reports revenue of {{ n.fanout_revenue|brl }} against {{ n.paid|brl }} actually paid ({{ ((n.fanout_revenue / n.paid - 1) * 100)|pct(0) }} too high).</li>
  <li><b>Analyst B</b> counted every order, including canceled and unavailable ones.</li>
  <li><b>Analyst C</b> counted delivered orders only: a valid metric, but a different one under the same name.</li>
</ul>
<p>So there were two problems: <b>grain errors</b> (joins that multiply rows), solved by modelling and tests, and <b>definition drift</b> (one word, several meanings), solved by agreeing on named definitions.</p>

<h2 id="data">2. Data</h2>
<p>The Kaggle release <code>olistbr/brazilian-ecommerce</code>: 9 CSV files from a Brazilian marketplace, orders from 2016-09-04 to 2018-10-17. They are loaded unchanged (every column as text) into DuckDB by <code>ingestion/load_raw.py</code>, which records an audit of every file:</p>
<div class="scroll">{{ tables.audit | safe }}</div>
<p class="note">Reviews have more physical lines than rows (comments contain line breaks), so the loader records both counts. Raw files are never committed to the repository.</p>

<h2 id="architecture">3. Architecture and modelling</h2>
<div class="svgwrap">{{ architecture_svg | safe }}</div>
<ul>
  <li><b>staging</b> ({{ stats.models_by_layer['staging'] }} views): one per raw table; rename, cast, clean, de-duplicate. No joins.</li>
  <li><b>intermediate</b> ({{ stats.models_by_layer['intermediate'] }} views): roll items, payments and reviews up to one row per order <i>before</i> joining, so nothing can fan out; order status and lifecycle logic; person-level customers.</li>
  <li><b>marts</b>: a star schema (<code>fct_orders</code>, <code>fct_order_items</code>, <code>dim_customers</code>, <code>dim_products</code>, <code>dim_sellers</code>, <code>dim_date</code>) plus two wide tables (<code>customer_360</code>, <code>seller_scorecard</code>). Contracts are enforced and the marts are the only public layer.</li>
  <li><b>Also included:</b> {{ stats.seeds }} seeds (the order-status definition among them), an SCD2 snapshot of order status, a semantic layer ({{ stats.metrics }} MetricFlow metrics), and {{ stats.exposures }} exposures (this app and the analyses).</li>
</ul>
<h3>The star schema analysts query</h3>
<div class="svgwrap">{{ star_svg | safe }}</div>
{% if lineage_png %}<h3>Lineage as dbt docs draws it</h3><img class="shot" src="{{ lineage_png }}" alt="dbt docs lineage graph">{% endif %}
