</div>
<div class="grid2">
  <div class="fig">{{ figs.tests_cat | safe }}</div>
  <div class="fig">{{ figs.tests_layer | safe }}</div>
</div>
<h3>Known-issue baselines</h3>
<p>A raw problem we cannot fix ourselves is a test that passes at today's count, warns if the count grows, and fails if it explodes. Failing rows are stored for inspection. CI plants every one of these issues in a synthetic dataset and checks that each test detects it.</p>
<div class="scroll">{{ tables.known | safe }}</div>
{% if inc %}
<h3>Incremental model check</h3>
<p><code>fct_order_items</code> is built incrementally. <code>scripts/check_incremental.py</code> loads the data as it looked on {{ inc.cutoff }}, then the full data:</p>
<ul>
  <li>The incremental run added the new orders and updated {{ inc.scenario_a.older_rows_whose_status_changed|int }} rows whose order changed status, {{ inc.scenario_a.of_which_outside_lookback_window|int }} of them older than the lookback window.</li>
  <li>Running it again changed nothing, and a full rebuild produced an identical table (row-level checksum: {{ 'match' if inc.scenario_a.incremental_equals_full_refresh else 'MISMATCH' }}).</li>
  <li>In the late-data scenario, {{ inc.scenario_b.rows_missed_by_incremental }} rows were missed by design. The grain test caught it, and a full refresh repaired it.</li>
</ul>
{% endif %}

<h2 id="result">6. The result: one order count</h2>
<div class="fig" id="fig-paths">{{ figs.paths | safe }}</div>
<div class="scroll">{{ tables.named | safe }}</div>
<p>The same check runs in every <code>dbt build</code>, with six paths, including one computed straight from staging and the status seed, skipping every intermediate and mart model. A regression therefore fails CI before it reaches a report.</p>

<h2 id="q1">7. Q1 · How do delivery time and lateness relate to bad reviews?</h2>
<div class="question">Decision supported: where logistics improvements should start. Population: delivered orders with a delivery date and a review, complete months.</div>
<div class="fig" id="fig-q1a">{{ figs.q1a | safe }}</div>
<div class="fig" id="fig-q1b">{{ figs.q1b | safe }}</div>
<div class="fig" id="fig-q1c">{{ figs.q1c | safe }}</div>
<ul>
  <li><b>Late vs on time.</b> Late deliveries get a 1-2 star review {{ n.bad_late|pct }} of the time (95% CI {{ n.bad_late_lo|pct }}-{{ n.bad_late_hi|pct }}) vs {{ n.bad_ok|pct }} on time ({{ n.bad_ok_lo|pct }}-{{ n.bad_ok_hi|pct }}).</li>
  <li><b>The jump is immediate.</b> It happens as soon as the parcel misses its estimate.</li>
  <li><b>States.</b> Across {{ n.states_n }} states, late rate and bad-review rate correlate at r = {{ '%.2f'|format(n.corr) }}; lateness alone explains {{ (n.r2 * 100)|pct(0) }} of the spread. {{ n.worst_states }} are late most often. The 95% interval of {{ n.excess_states }} sits wholly above what their lateness predicts, and only RJ's excess survives once delivery time is also accounted for.</li>
  <li><b>Caveat.</b> This is an association: remote states also differ in products, prices and carriers.</li>
</ul>
<div class="scroll">{{ tables.states | safe }}</div>

<h2 id="q2">8. Q2 · What share of customers buy again, and how quickly?</h2>
<div class="question">Decision supported: retention vs acquisition budget, and which first-order experiences to fix.</div>
<div class="fig" id="fig-q2a">{{ figs.q2a | safe }}</div>
<div class="grid2">
  <div class="fig">{{ figs.q2b | safe }}</div>
  <div class="fig">{{ figs.q2d | safe }}</div>
</div>
<div class="fig">{{ figs.q2c | safe }}</div>
<ul>
  <li><b>Repeat buying is rare.</b> {{ n.repeat|pct(2) }} of customers ever buy again on a different day.</li>
  <li><b>The definition matters.</b> Counting split checkouts as repeats would report {{ n.repeat_orders|pct(2) }}.</li>
  <li><b>Return is slow.</b> Repeaters take a median of {{ '%.0f'|format(n.median_second) }} days, and that figure is biased toward quick returns. For the customers observed for a full year it is {{ '%.0f'|format(n.median_full_year) }} days among those who came back within the year (<code>analyses/q2_repeat_purchase.md</code>).</li>
  <li><b>Fair cohort comparison.</b> Cohorts are compared only once every customer has had the full window to return.</li>
  <li><b>First experience.</b> Late or badly reviewed first orders go with lower repurchase, but the intervals are wide: a hypothesis to test, not a result.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="q3">9. Q3 · How concentrated is GMV across sellers, and how do sellers differ?</h2>
<div class="question">Decision supported: which sellers the seller-management team calls first.</div>
<div class="grid2">
  <div class="fig" id="fig-q3a">{{ figs.q3a | safe }}</div>
  <div class="fig" id="fig-q3b">{{ figs.q3b | safe }}</div>
</div>
<div class="fig">{{ figs.q3c | safe }}</div>
<ul>
  <li><b>Concentration.</b> The top 5% / 10% / 20% of active sellers hold {{ n.top5|pct }} / {{ n.top10|pct }} / {{ n.top20|pct }} of net GMV.</li>
  <li><b>Handover.</b> When a seller hands the parcel to the carrier after its own deadline, {{ n.hv_late|pct }} of orders arrive late vs {{ n.hv_ok|pct }} otherwise ({{ '%.1f'|format(n.hv_ratio) }}x; single-seller orders).</li>
  <li><b>The call list.</b> {{ n.risk_sellers }} established sellers (50+ net orders) exceed both thresholds (late handover > 15%, bad reviews > 20%) and hold {{ n.risk_share|pct }} of marketplace GMV.</li>
</ul>
