<h2 id="recommendations">11. Recommendations</h2>
<div class="scroll"><table class="data">
<tr><th>Who</th><th>What</th><th>Expected impact</th><th>How we measure</th><th>Risk</th></tr>
<tr><td>Analytics Lead + Finance</td><td>Adopt net orders as the headline order KPI; report placed and delivered under their own names</td><td>One order number in every report</td><td>Conflicting order counts in business reviews: zero; the 6-path dbt test stays green</td><td>Finance may prefer delivered orders: a status-seed change plus a full refresh (ADR 0002)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Analysts</td><td>Query the marts only; retire raw-table queries and dashboards</td><td>No fan-out or definition errors</td><td>Share of dashboards reading marts</td><td>Migration effort for legacy reports</td></tr>
<tr><td>Logistics</td><td>Review carriers and routes in RJ first (second-most late deliveries, and the one state with clearly more bad reviews than its lateness predicts); in {{ n.worst_states }} make the delivery estimate route-specific</td><td>Fewer late deliveries; at most ~53 fewer bad reviews per 100 late deliveries avoided (upper bound)</td><td>Monthly late and bad-review rate, RJ vs MG and SP; the Northeast before vs after</td><td>RJ does worse even on time; a later promise can cost sales</td></tr>
<tr><td>Seller management</td><td>Work the {{ n.risk_sellers }} sellers above both thresholds, the {{ n.clear_sellers }} that clearly exceed both first</td><td>Lower late-handover rates</td><td>Their rates vs flagged sellers not yet contacted</td><td>Some flags are chance; {{ n.late_after_ok_handover|pct(0) }} of late deliveries follow an on-time handover</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRM</td><td>Pilot a second-purchase offer 30-90 days after the first delivery, with a random no-offer group</td><td>A measured, not assumed, lift on a ~2% base</td><td>180-day repeat rate, pilot vs no-offer group</td><td>A 2% base needs a large pilot</td></tr>
<tr><td>Data platform</td><td>Ask the source owner for refunds, quantities and time zones</td><td>Net revenue and true unit counts</td><td>New source tables available and tested</td><td>Depends on the source team's roadmap</td></tr>
</table></div>

<h2 id="limitations">12. Limitations</h2>
<ul>
  <li><b>Historical data.</b> The data covers 2016 to 2018 from one marketplace, and only 2017-01 to 2018-08 is complete. Recency is measured against the last purchase date in the data.</li>
  <li><b>Associations, not causes.</b> All business findings are associations. Experiments or holdouts are needed before acting at scale.</li>
  <li><b>No refunds.</b> The source has no refund data, so revenue is gross merchandise value, not net revenue.</li>
  <li><b>Synthetic CI data.</b> CI runs on a synthetic dataset with the same schema, because raw data cannot be committed. Full-volume validation is run locally (<code>make all</code>).</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="reproduce">13. How to reproduce</h2>
<p>Download the 9 CSVs from Kaggle and point <code>OLIST_RAW_DIR</code> (or <code>config.yaml</code>) at the folder. Then run <code>uv sync</code>, <code>make all</code> and <code>uv run streamlit run dashboard/app.py</code>.</p>
<p>Where things live:</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>dbt/</code> holds the models, tests, seeds and snapshot.</li>
  <li><code>sql/</code> and <code>analyses/</code> hold standalone SQL.</li>
  <li><code>notebooks/</code> holds three notebooks, exported to <code>reports/*.html</code>.</li>
  <li><code>docs/</code> holds the request, metric definitions, data dictionary, DQ report, analyst guide and ADRs.</li>
</ul>
